## Summary

Start the SchemaRouter 0.4 feature line with a native LangGraph integration.

- add `schemarouter[langgraph]` with `langgraph>=1.2,<2`;
- expose `to_langgraph_node()` as a sync/async `RunnableLambda` accepted directly by `StateGraph.add_node()`;
- support either a default `query` + `arguments` state contract, a full `schemarouter_request`, or a custom `request_factory`;
- return checkpoint-friendly JSON partial-state updates by default, with an opt-in typed `ToolResult` mode;
- keep LangGraph runtime config separate from SchemaRouter `RunConfig` to avoid authority/config conflation;
- compile and execute real sync/async `StateGraph` tests plus a runnable example;
- certify the feature inside the existing blocking `langchain-integration` CI job so protected-main enforcement covers it without a new repository ruleset check;
- update English/Korean README, MkDocs, compatibility matrix, maturity matrix, and changelogs.

## Design boundary

LangGraph remains responsible for graph control flow, checkpointing, memory, and orchestration. SchemaRouter remains responsible for schema-aware planning, policy, approval/budgets, binding checks, and validated tool execution.

This follows the current LangGraph node contract: StateGraph nodes may be sync/async functions or Runnables, and functions are wrapped into RunnableLambda internally.

## Purpose
Finish the post-merge convergence between Intelligence Gateway V1 and the now-merged Free Resource Pool runtime-state/persistence contracts.

## Why
PR #19 merged Gateway V1 successfully, but subsequent Resource Pool work added the canonical `ResourcePoolStateManager`, `MemoryResourceStateStore` and `MemoryUsageEventStore`. Gateway outcomes still updated resource health directly in-memory and the docs still described overlapping provider-registry ownership.

## Changes
- make Resource Pool `eligible()` the authoritative schedulability gate before Gateway quality/latency scoring;
- route Gateway success/failure observations through `ResourcePoolStateManager`;
- preserve latency and retry cooldown observations in `ResourceStateSnapshot`;
- allow Gateway `UsageEvent` records to flow to a durable usage store as well as the in-memory reference ledger;
- clarify that ResourceRegistry owns scheduling/free/usage/health/quota while Gateway ProviderRegistry owns execution bindings and short-lived circuit/rate state;
- update Architecture V1, Gateway V1, Free Resource Pool V1 and Legacy Work Map accordingly;
- add tests for persistable resource outcomes and durable usage sinks without prompt leakage.

## Invariants
- no second resource database;
- no second accounting database;
- no prompt content in resource/accounting records;
- no provider name in the public routing contract;
- Cantiere remains Project/Execution lifecycle authority;
- real remote resources still fail closed while health is UNKNOWN.

## Audit finding

The repository's checked-in `pack/` tree had become a stale, partial resource pack: it still contained older generated `motion.vmd` / player adapter binaries but did not contain the newer generated Piercing and effect resources. CI artifacts were correct only because `build_pack.py` regenerated missing files before validation.

That creates two bad states:
- a fresh source checkout visually looks like it contains an installable `pack/`, but zipping it directly is incomplete;
- tracked generated binaries/docs can silently drift behind the generators and current root docs.

## Fix
- keep only static `pack/pack.mcmeta` in Git;
- ignore every other generated staging entry under `pack/`;
- make `build_pack.py` clean the staging tree before every build, preserving only `pack.mcmeta`;
- delete the stale tracked generated pack binaries/copies;
- add tests proving stale nested files are removed and missing static metadata fails closed;
- update development docs to make the staging/build contract explicit and correct the Piercing Noutou timing text.

The final CI ZIP contract is unchanged: the build regenerates all expected VMD/PMD/effect/icon files, copies README/license/notices, validates the staging tree, packages deterministically, and validates the ZIP again.

